## 2. Risk register by asset

| Asset | Type | Tier | Applicable CVEs | KEV | P(attack succeeds) | Loss events / yr | Loss per event | **ALE / yr** | P(≥1 loss / yr) |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
{% for a in r.assets %}
| {{ a.asset.name|md }} | {{ a.asset.type }} | {{ a.asset.sensitivity_tier }} | {{ a.scenario_count }} | {{ a.kev_count }} | {{ a.aggregate.p_any|pct }} | {{ a.expected_loss_events|num(3) }} | {{ a.aggregate.lm|money }} | **{{ a.total_ale|money }}** | {{ a.prob_loss_event|pct }} |
{% endfor %}

How to read this: an attacker needs only **one** working vulnerability. *P(attack succeeds)* is the
chance that at least one applicable CVE works: 1 − Π(1 − pᵢ). *Loss events / yr* = threat event
frequency for the asset × that probability. *Loss per event* is the average loss magnitude, weighted
toward the easiest-to-exploit CVEs. *P(≥1 loss / yr)* = 1 − e^(−loss events), treating events as a
Poisson process.

For comparison, simply summing every scenario's ALE would give {{ p.scenario_ale_sum|money }}/yr. That
treats each CVE as a separate, independent breach, which badly overstates assets with long vulnerability lists.

